{"compressedText":"# Field Guide: Preparing a Home for a 72-Hour Power Outage\n\nPrepare a household to pass safely and comfortably through an outage up to 72 hours. Temperate climate, no backup generator. Do sections in order; earlier ones matter most.\n\n## Water\n- Store 4 L per person per day = 12 L per person for the full 72 hours.\n- Half for drinking; half for cooking and basic hygiene.\n- Food-grade containers, out of direct sunlight; replace every 6 months.\n- If storm forecast with advance warning, also fill bathtub — bathtub water for flushing toilets and washing, never drinking.\n\n## Food\n- Dedicated shelf, non-perishable food for 3 days: canned beans, canned fish, peanut butter, crackers, dried fruit, shelf-stable milk.\n- Choose no-cook foods; own a manual can opener (electric useless).\n- Eat order: perishable fridge food first, then freezer, then emergency shelf.\n\n## Refrigerator and freezer\n- Unopened fridge keeps food safe ~4 hours.\n- Full freezer holds safe temperature ~48 hours; half-full only ~24 hours — keep it consolidated and full, even with containers of frozen water.\n- Keep both doors closed as much as possible; tape a reminder note to each.\n- Discard perishable food over 2 hours above 4°C; when in doubt, throw it out.\n\n## Light\n- Primary light: battery-powered LED lanterns or headlamps. Avoid candles entirely — leading cause of house fires during outages.\n- At least one light per person, plus two spare battery sets per light.\n- One headlamp beside each bed, one lantern in kitchen; place where findable in the dark.\n\n## Heat and safety\n- Never run camping stove, barbecue, or generator indoors, in garage, or near window — each emits carbon monoxide, odourless, can be fatal.\n- Install at least one battery-powered carbon monoxide alarm on each floor where people sleep.\n- In cold, pick one small room on south-facing side, close door, gather everyone; layered clothing and blankets beat any improvised heater.\n- If indoor temperature falls below 10°C and you cannot stay warm, relocate to a warming centre or neighbour's home.\n\n## Communication and information\n- Keep battery-powered or hand-crank radio; learn local emergency station's frequency before outage.\n- Charge every phone and power bank whenever storm forecast; full 20,000 mAh power bank recharges typical phone ~4 times.\n- Put phone in low-power mode at start.\n- Agree in advance on one out-of-area contact everyone texts — texts beat voice calls on congested networks.\n\n## Medical needs\n- First-aid kit and 7-day supply of every prescription; rotate to stay in date.\n- Refrigerated medication such as insulin generally lasts at room temperature up to 28 days — confirm your specific drug with a pharmacist beforehand.\n- Write pharmacy, doctor, and poison-control numbers on paper; a dead phone is not a phone book.\n\n## When the power returns\n- Wait 5 minutes before switching major appliances on, letting grid stabilise.\n- Check freezer: food still holding ice crystals can be safely refrozen.\n- Restock everything used within one week, starting with water and batteries.\n- Write a one-page note on what was missing or awkward; fix at least one gap before next outage.","expectedMetric":521,"hardConstraintNote":"Still a fully self-contained instruction document (GR-3): retains the title, the framing assumptions (up-to-72 h outage, temperate climate, no generator, do-in-order), all 8 section headings, and every actionable instruction with its exact figures and units. A reader with only this text can execute the entire preparation unaided — nothing was outsourced to the original, only reworded denser. No frontier number is asserted in prose except by reference to frontier.json (GR-1).","killedLeverCitations":["notes/KILLED_LEVERS.md contains no K-ids (only the placeholder '— none yet' row), so no killed lever is re-proposed and no new evidence is required (GR-6)."],"lens":"restructurer","leverId":"restructure-to-telegraphic-bullets","rationale":"The original is 8 ordered sections of connective-heavy instructional prose. Structure-level compression converts each section to telegraphic bullets: articles (a/the/an) and filler connectives dropped, the multi-sentence preamble merged into one line, safety asides kept inline via em-dashes. Every number, unit, item list, temperature, duration, and ordered step is preserved verbatim (4 L/person/day, 12 L/72 h, half drink/half cook+hygiene, food-grade+no sunlight+replace 6 months, bathtub-if-forecast/never drink; 3-day shelf + 6 named foods + manual can opener + eat-order fridge>freezer>shelf; fridge ~4 h, full freezer ~48 h, half-full ~24 h, doors closed + note each, discard >2 h above 4°C; LED lanterns/headlamps not candles, 1 light/person + 2 spare battery sets/light, headlamp/bed + lantern/kitchen; no stove/BBQ/generator indoors/garage/window = CO odourless/fatal, 1 CO alarm/sleeping floor, south room + layers > heater, below 10°C relocate; radio + station frequency, 20,000 mAh = ~4 charges, low-power mode, one out-of-area text contact; first-aid + 7-day meds + rotate, insulin ~28 days room temp confirm pharmacist, write pharmacy/doctor/poison-control on paper; return: wait 5 min, ice-crystal refreeze, restock 1 week water+batteries first, one-page note fix 1 gap). I deliberately stopped at ~521 words rather than the ~410 restructurer ceiling: I am blind to which 8 facts the Gap probes (T2/GR-4), so preserving 100% of content is the only gate-safe strategy; a denser cut trades held-out comprehension risk for marginal word savings and is not worth the multiplicative-gate collapse (T5).","title":"Collapse all 8 prose sections into terse imperative bullet directives"}